#!/usr/bin/env php
<?php

declare(strict_types=1);

/*
 * ================================================================================
 * THE SENTINEL THEME, ON THE DEMO RESTAURANT, THROUGH THE REAL PUBLISHER — QRPAGER-49A
 * ================================================================================
 *
 * `scan-css` and `QRPAGER-46`'s emitted/referenced closure are SOURCE-level guards: they
 * prove **no literal colour is written**. Neither proves **the theme reaches the rendered
 * pixel**, which is a different claim about a different artefact — the bytes a visitor
 * receives, resolved by a browser. This script produces the subject of that claim.
 *
 * ================================================================================
 * ⚠⚠ WHICH DATABASE ROLE THIS RUNS AS, WHICH PHASE 1'S DOCBLOCK GOT WRONG
 * ================================================================================
 *
 * Phase 1 said this script *"writes as the database owner"*. **It does not, and Muhammad's
 * review of 2026-11-14 asked precisely the question that exposed it.** The sentence was carried
 * over from `bin/publish-landing-fixture`'s header; the code never opens an owner connection.
 *
 * Every statement runs through `LandingPages`, and every `LandingPages` read and write opens
 * `withTenant($tenantId, …)` with **no connection argument**, which
 * `TenantContext::run()` resolves to `DatabaseRoles::connection('app')` — the APP role, the one
 * the web process holds, under FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY, with `app.tenant_id` set per
 * transaction. So:
 *
 *   - **It sets tenant context; it does not use a bypass.** There is no `withoutTenantScope()`
 *     in this file and no `pgsql_owner` anywhere in it. `§4 › Tenancy` allows exactly those two
 *     routes and this takes the first.
 *   - **Under RLS the app role sees only this tenant's rows**, so the question *"the owner sees
 *     no rows either"* does not arise: this is not the owner.
 *   - **No statement it reaches is a bulk `UPDATE` or `DELETE`** (`§4 › Postgres traps`). Its
 *     one statement of its own is `select name from tenants where id = ?`; the writes are
 *     `LandingPages::saveDraft()`'s `UPDATE … WHERE tenant_id = ?` and `publish()`'s
 *     `UPDATE … WHERE tenant_id = ? AND status = ? AND published_at is not distinct from ?`.
 *     Each names a column of its own table, and there is no `DELETE` on the path.
 *   - `SentinelThemeScriptTest` asserts the first and third of those against this file's own
 *     source, so a later edit that opened an owner connection fails a test rather than
 *     contradicting this paragraph.
 *
 * (`php artisan studio:demo-tenant`, which the orchestrator runs to seed and to restore, is a
 * different program and DOES write as the owner — through `withTenant($id, …, 'pgsql_owner')`,
 * per tenant, which is `§2.10`'s first pattern. It is `QRPAGER-48`'s and is unchanged.)
 *
 * ================================================================================
 * WHY IT IS SAFE TO RUN, AND WHY `FixtureScriptGuardTest` ADMITS IT
 * ================================================================================
 *
 * Three independent halves, because a control that is only correct while its caller is correct
 * is not a control:
 *
 *   1. **Environment.** It refuses unless `APP_ENV` is `local` or `testing`, failing CLOSED on
 *      an unset or unrecognised value, before it touches the database or the bucket.
 *   2. **Identity.** It takes NO tenant argument. The tenant is `SeedDemoTenantCommand::
 *      TENANT_ID`, a fixed uuid, so a caller cannot aim it at another restaurant — and it
 *      REFUSES an extra argument rather than ignoring one, so a caller who tries learns that
 *      it was not honoured instead of believing it was. A production database has no such
 *      tenant unless somebody ran the demo seeder there, and that seeder carries the same
 *      environment guard.
 *   3. **Role.** The app role under RLS, per the section above: even a mistake here could not
 *      read or write a second tenant's rows.
 *
 * `FixtureScriptGuardTest`'s exact set is a DERIVED list of the scripts that carry the guard,
 *  * held exact so a writer cannot be added without a reader seeing it. Admitting this script is
 * safe because it carries the guard and the guard is real, and because of what DRIVES it:
 * `FixtureScriptGuardTest` runs it under `production` and requires exit 1; and
 * `SentinelThemeScriptTest` runs it under `staging`, `Local`, `prod` and `development` as well —
 * ⚠ that second half was MISSING and a first draft of this paragraph claimed it: the generic
 * fail-closed loop in `FixtureScriptGuardTest` runs those four over `bin/issue-pager-session`
 * ONLY, so for this script the evidence was `production` alone (`§11` QA M7, Security 4). An
 * UNSET `APP_ENV` is not driven by any test: a child process reads `.env`, which supplies
 * `local`, so the case cannot be built; the code defers to `config/app.php`, whose default is
 * `production`. It also asserts that a refusal leaves this tenant's row and its published pointer
 * byte-identical, which the generic case does not, since it counts `users` and `tenants` and this
 * script writes neither.
 *
 * ================================================================================
 * WHAT IT DOES
 * ================================================================================
 *
 * ⚠ **A REAL THEME ON A REAL TENANT, PUBLISHED BY `LandingPages::publish()`.** Not a test
 * fixture, not a stubbed resolver, not `LandingRenderer` called directly: the six values are
 * written to `landing_pages.draft_document` through `saveDraft()` — the same call
 * `ThemePage::save()` makes — and then published through the same phases a restaurant's own
 * Publish button runs. A harness that rendered the page itself would be measuring its own idea
 * of the path.
 *
 * ⚠ **THE DEMO RESTAURANT**, `QRPAGER-48`'s ruling — *"an e2e fixture must never again be the
 * thing I am asked to judge a design from"* — applied to a measurement of the published page
 * too: it carries all seven block types and its own imagery, so every colour-bearing element
 * the renderer can produce is on the page.
 *
 * ⚠ **ONE DOCUMENT, UNCHANGED BUT FOR TWO MEMBERS.** The stored draft is read, `template` and
 * `theme` are replaced, and everything else — every block, every media reference, the SEO, the
 * logo — is written back exactly as it was. The invariant is *one unchanged tenant document
 * under every arrangement*, and a document this script composed would measure this script.
 *
 * ⚠ **IT RESTORES NOTHING, DELIBERATELY.** When it returns, the demo restaurant's live page
 * carries the sentinel theme. `tools/gate/bin/theme-paint-proof` restores it by re-running
 * `php artisan studio:demo-tenant`, which is idempotent and is the only thing that knows what
 * the demo restaurant's own theme is.
 *
 * USAGE:  php bin/publish-sentinel-theme --templates
 *         php bin/publish-sentinel-theme <template>
 * PRINTS: `--templates`  one JSON line: every `LandingTemplate` case, from the enum
 *         otherwise      one JSON line: {"template":"…","hostname":"…","checksum":"…", …}
 */

use App\Domain\Landing\LandingDocumentInvalid;
use App\Domain\Landing\LandingTemplate;
use App\Features\Landing\Console\SeedDemoTenantCommand;
use App\Features\Landing\LandingPages;
use App\Features\Landing\PublishFailed;
use Illuminate\Contracts\Console\Kernel;
use Illuminate\Foundation\Application;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\DB;
use Tests\Support\SentinelTheme;

require __DIR__.'/../vendor/autoload.php';

/** @var Application $app */
$app = require __DIR__.'/../bootstrap/app.php';
$app->make(Kernel::class)->bootstrap();

if (! app()->environment('local', 'testing')) {
    fwrite(
        STDERR,
        "refusing to run: this publishes to the real object store and is for local and testing only.\n"
        ."  APP_ENV is '".(string) config('app.env')."'.\n"
    );

    exit(1);
}

if (! class_exists(SentinelTheme::class)) {
    fwrite(STDERR, "refusing to run: Tests\\Support\\SentinelTheme is not loadable; install dev dependencies.\n");

    exit(1);
}

/*
 * ⚠ THE SENTINELS COVER EVERY COLOUR ROLE, AND A GAP IS REFUSED RATHER THAN MEASURED AROUND.
 * A colour token added to `ThemeTokens::NAMES` with no sentinel would be a studio picker this
 * harness never exercises — a dead control it could not even report. Refused here as well as in
 * `SentinelThemeTest`, because this is what a run actually executes.
 */
$sentinels = SentinelTheme::values();
$roles = SentinelTheme::colourRoles();

if (array_keys($sentinels) !== $roles) {
    fwrite(
        STDERR,
        "refusing to run: the sentinel table does not cover the contract's colour roles.\n"
        .'  contract: '.implode(', ', $roles)."\n"
        .'  sentinel: '.implode(', ', array_keys($sentinels))."\n"
    );

    exit(1);
}

/*
 * ⚠ THE ENUM IS THE LIST, AND THIS MODE EXISTS SO NOTHING DOWNSTREAM HOLDS A SECOND ONE.
 * `browser/landing-templates.spec.ts` carries `['rail', 'ember', 'tile']` as a literal, which
 * is a check whose scope excludes exactly where the next damage lands: `QRPAGER-49` adds three
 * redesigns. The orchestrator asks for this list rather than spelling one.
 */
if (($argv[1] ?? '') === '--templates') {
    echo json_encode(LandingTemplate::values(), JSON_THROW_ON_ERROR), "\n";

    exit(0);
}

/*
 * ⚠ AN EXTRA ARGUMENT IS REFUSED, NOT IGNORED. The natural thing to try is a tenant uuid, and
 * a script that silently ignored it would let the caller believe it had been honoured. It is
 * not: the tenant is fixed below.
 */
if (count($argv) !== 2) {
    fwrite(
        STDERR,
        "refusing to run: exactly one argument is accepted, a template; this script takes no tenant.\n"
        .'  the templates are: '.implode(', ', LandingTemplate::values())."\n"
    );

    exit(1);
}

$template = LandingTemplate::tryFrom((string) $argv[1]);

if ($template === null) {
    fwrite(
        STDERR,
        "refusing to run: '".(string) $argv[1]."' is not a LandingTemplate case.\n"
        .'  the cases are: '.implode(', ', LandingTemplate::values())."\n"
    );

    exit(1);
}

$tenantId = SeedDemoTenantCommand::TENANT_ID;


/*
 * ⚠ **A REFUSAL MUST BE A NON-ZERO EXIT.** An uncaught `PublishFailed` is rendered by
 * Laravel's handler and the process exits 0, so a caller reading the status would treat a
 * refused publish as a published page — `bin/publish-landing-fixture` paid for this one
 * already and its note is why this is here rather than discovered again.
 *
 * ⚠⚠ AND THE MESSAGE IS PRINTED ONLY FOR THE TWO TYPES WHOSE MESSAGES ARE WRITTEN FOR A READER
 * (`§11` Security 5). This used to print `$e->getMessage()` of ANY Throwable, and a
 * `QueryException`'s message carries the SQL and its bound parameters — here, the draft document
 * as JSON — into stderr, which `theme-paint-proof` tees into a transcript this ticket commits. `§4 ›
 * Edge & ops`: *"stack traces never carry SQL or bound parameters"*. Anything else prints its class
 * and is `report()`ed, so the log — where redaction applies — has the detail.
 */
$refuse = static function (Throwable $e): never {
    $safe = $e instanceof PublishFailed || $e instanceof LandingDocumentInvalid;

    fwrite(
        STDERR,
        'refused: '.$e::class.($safe
            ? ': '.$e->getMessage()
            : ' (message withheld: it can carry SQL and bound parameters; the detail is in the application log)')."\n"
    );

    if (! $safe) {
        try {
            report($e);
        } catch (Throwable) {
            // a failing logger must not turn a refusal into a different failure
        }
    }

    exit(1);
};

try {
    $name = withTenant($tenantId, fn (): ?string => DB::table('tenants')->where('id', $tenantId)->value('name'));
} catch (Throwable $e) {
    $refuse($e);
}

if (! is_string($name) || $name === '') {
    fwrite(
        STDERR,
        "refusing to run: the demo restaurant is not seeded.\n"
        ."  run `php artisan studio:demo-tenant` first — this measures that tenant and no other.\n"
    );

    exit(1);
}

$pages = $app->make(LandingPages::class);

try {
    $document = $pages->draft($tenantId)->toArray();
} catch (Throwable $e) {
    $refuse($e);
}

$document['template'] = $template->value;
$document['theme'] = $sentinels;

try {
    $pages->saveDraft($tenantId, $document);
    $published = $pages->publish($tenantId);
} catch (Throwable $e) {
    $refuse($e);
}

echo json_encode([
    'tenant_id' => $tenantId,
    'tenant_name' => $name,
    'template' => $template->value,
    'hostname' => $published->hostname,
    'checksum' => $published->checksum,
    'byte_size' => $published->byteSize,
    'asset_count' => count($published->assetKeys),
    'published_at' => $published->publishedAt,
    'theme' => $sentinels,
], JSON_THROW_ON_ERROR | JSON_UNESCAPED_SLASHES | JSON_UNESCAPED_UNICODE), "\n";
